#!/bin/sh
# stage: the release flow's three scripted stages. Run it as the cmd of an
# exec group routed for build, verify and publish (one group with the stage
# in cmd: contrib/release/stage $YARDR_STAGE); docs/install.md "Making a
# release" says how a release is cut with the yard, and docs/setup.md how
# the yardmaster adds the group and the routes.
#
#   contrib/release/stage <build|verify|publish>
#
# It reads the bead from the environment the exec runner gives it (YARDR_BEAD,
# YARDR_DEPOT_PATH, YARDR_BASE, YARDR_BIN), like contrib/deploy/deploy-dokku,
# and the version from the bead's title's last word, which must be
# v<major>.<minor>.<patch>; the bead's body is the release notes.
#
#   build: refuses a bad title (question: the bead is wrong), a published
#     release, a tag at another commit, a dirty tree, a checkout that is
#     not the remote's base, and a machine that is not set up to sign
#     (YARDR_RELEASE_SIGN_IDENTITY and YARDR_RELEASE_NOTARY_PROFILE, both,
#     below), all before anything is tagged; tags the base commit, pushes
#     the tag, and waits for the draft release CI's release workflow makes
#     for the tag, bounded: the two linux archives and checksums.txt; if
#     those sums name install.sh, it waits for that asset too. It then builds
#     the two darwin archives on this machine, signed with the
#     Developer ID and notarized by Apple (contrib/release/build, with
#     SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH from the tagged commit, as the workflow sets it),
#     makes checksums.txt name all four archives and install.sh, and
#     uploads the darwin archives and that checksums.txt to the draft.
#     Then it sets the draft's notes from the bead's body and notes the
#     five signing commands for the version on the bead. Then done, to
#     sign. A run that died after tagging resumes: a tag at the base
#     commit with no release on it is pushed again and waited on, and a
#     draft already standing is adopted, not refused, and gets its darwin
#     archives built and uploaded again. A CI run that concluded anything
#     but success reports failed with the run's URL.
#     When GitHub Actions is switched off on the release repo there is no
#     CI to wait for: build runs contrib/release/build on this machine
#     instead, for all four archives (darwin signed and notarized as
#     above, SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH from the tagged commit, as the workflow
#     sets it) and makes the draft from it, with no attestation; a draft
#     already standing is adopted in both modes. The draft is the build's
#     archives and checksums.txt and, when the build wrote one, its
#     install.sh.
#   verify: downloads the release, checks checksums.txt.minisig against the
#     release key, the archives against checksums.txt, and then each
#     archive by who built it: a linux archive's build-provenance
#     attestation (gh attestation verify), skipped with no attestation
#     when Actions is off on the release repo, and a darwin archive's
#     signature, in place of an attestation, which a build on this machine
#     has none of: its yardr must pass codesign --verify, be signed by
#     YARDR_RELEASE_SIGN_IDENTITY and be accepted by Gatekeeper as a
#     notarized Developer ID binary (spctl), with or without Actions. Then
#     done, to publish; a missing or bad signature reports failed with what
#     minisign said, so sign is asked again, and archives that do not match,
#     an attestation that does not verify, a darwin binary that is not signed
#     and notarized, or a check that could not run, report question, to
#     decide. An install.sh on the release is no archive: it is not counted
#     with them and has no attestation. The checksums name it (a release
#     made before they did has no line for it, and passes with a note saying
#     so), and it is held to the tag besides: it must be the tagged commit's
#     own file, byte for byte, and a release without one is questioned where
#     the tag has one.
#   publish: releases the draft, files the yardr.dev sync bead and advances
#     it to new, notes its id on the release bead. Then done, to published.
#
# Every failure path ends in a done with an outcome or a hold, never a bare
# exit, as deploy-dokku documents: has_outcome asks prime before moving, so
# only moves the flow allows are tried, and a flow that names none holds
# the bead instead.
#
# Needs on PATH: jq and git always; gh for build, verify and publish; go,
# codesign, zip and xcrun (a Mac's, with the Xcode command line tools) for
# build, which signs and notarizes the darwin binaries and needs this Mac
# logged in, with the keychain that holds the identity unlocked; minisign,
# shasum (or sha256sum), tar, codesign and spctl for verify. gh acts as
# whoever it is logged in as.
#
# Another depot's release flow can call this same script with its own release:
# the repo and the key come from the environment, with yardr's as the
# defaults. Its CI is expected to draft the release the same way
# (.github/workflows/release.yml does): the two linux archives and
# checksums.txt on the pushed tag, each archive attested; the darwin archives
# are built here, so its contrib/release/build takes darwin targets and the
# signing variables below as yardr's does. With Actions off on that repo
# too the draft is built on this machine instead, with no attestation.
#
# The darwin binaries are signed under the one identity contrib/release/build
# is given, and the exec group's env_file is where it and the notary profile
# are named (docs/install.md, "Signed darwin binaries"): build refuses
# without both, before it tags, and verify holds each darwin binary to the
# identity.
#
#   YARDR_RELEASE_SIGN_IDENTITY  the "Developer ID Application" identity,
#                           whole, as `security find-identity -v -p
#                           codesigning` prints it between the quotes
#                           (no default: build refuses without it)
#   YARDR_RELEASE_NOTARY_PROFILE  the notarytool keychain profile the
#                           binaries are notarized under (no default)
#   YARDR_RELEASE_NOTARY_TIMEOUT  how long build waits for Apple's answer
#                           for each darwin binary: a whole number above
#                           zero with an optional s, m or h (default 30m)
#   YARDR_RELEASE_REPO      the GitHub repo, owner/name (default yardr-dev/yardr)
#   YARDR_RELEASE_PUBKEY    the minisign public key verify checks against
#                           (default yardr's release key)
#   YARDR_RELEASE_KEY_PATH  the signer's secret key, as the five commands
#                           name it (default ~/.minisign/<product>-release.key)
#   YARDR_RELEASE_REMOTE    the git remote the tag is fetched from and pushed
#                           to (default origin)
#   YARDR_RELEASE_WAIT_ROUNDS  how many times build asks after the draft
#                           (default 60)
#   YARDR_RELEASE_WAIT_SLEEP   seconds between those rounds (default 30)
set -u
# The ranges in the patterns below are ASCII's, and what git and gh say is
# read in English.
export LC_ALL=C
MODE=${1:-}
: "${YARDR_BEAD:?not run by an exec group}"
Y=${YARDR_BIN:-yardr}
BEAD=$YARDR_BEAD
REPO=${YARDR_DEPOT_PATH:-}
BASE=${YARDR_BASE:-}
GHREPO=${YARDR_RELEASE_REPO:-yardr-dev/yardr}
PRODUCT=${GHREPO##*/}
PUBKEY=${YARDR_RELEASE_PUBKEY:-RWSzUPGBbv7MDqH+nnbNmYdOm20UTkuk7eC5e6XuVtYRwZtaEZRlV/WB}
# The default keeps its ~ on every shell: quoted, it expands for whoever
# pastes the commands, not here (bash would expand an unquoted one, dash
# would not, and the note must read the same everywhere).
KEYPATH=${YARDR_RELEASE_KEY_PATH:-"~/.minisign/$PRODUCT-release.key"}
REMOTE=${YARDR_RELEASE_REMOTE:-origin}

# has_outcome <name>: whether the flow offers outcome <name> from here, asked
# of prime before moving, as merge-local asks (no jq: only sh, git and yardr
# are promised for the asking), so that only moves the flow allows are tried.
has_outcome() {
  "$Y" prime --bead "$BEAD" --json |
    sed -n '/^  "next": {/,/^  }/p' |
    sed -n '/^    "outcomes": \[/,/^    \]/p' |
    grep -q "^        \"name\": \"$1\""
}
# Each outcome is one move with its note. Moving ends this session (the
# daemon stops the script), so it is always the last thing done.
# done_with <stage> <note>: the flow's done outcome, else the stage by name.
done_with() {
  echo "== $BEAD: $2"
  if has_outcome "done"; then
    printf '%s\n' "$2" | "$Y" bead "done" "$BEAD" --note - && exit 0
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "$2" | "$Y" bead advance "$BEAD" --to "$1" --note -
  exit 0
}
hold() {
  echo "== hold $BEAD: $1"
  "$Y" bead note "$BEAD" "$MODE: $1"
  "$Y" bead hold "$BEAD"
  exit 0
}
# fail <outcome> <one line> [<more>]: the flow's outcome with the whole
# note; a flow that names none holds the bead instead.
fail() {
  outcome=$1
  text=$2
  if [ $# -gt 2 ]; then
    text="$text
$3"
  fi
  echo "== $BEAD $outcome: $2"
  if has_outcome "$outcome"; then
    printf '%s\n' "$MODE: $text" | "$Y" bead "done" "$BEAD" --outcome "$outcome" --note - && exit 0
  fi
  hold "$text"
}

case "$MODE" in
build | verify | publish) ;;
*)
  hold "run as the cmd of an exec group with the stage in it (contrib/release/stage \$YARDR_STAGE), not as '$*'"
  ;;
esac
# A dir depot has no tags and no base to build; a route that hands one over
# is a mistake in the depot's flow, as it is for merge-local.
if [ "${YARDR_DEPOT_KIND:-git}" = dir ]; then
  hold "depot ${YARDR_DEPOT:-?} is a dir depot, with no tags to release; route its stages to other groups"
fi
if ! command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  fail failed "jq is not on PATH; the release group runs where jq, git and gh are"
fi

tmp=""
cleanup() {
  [ -z "$tmp" ] || rm -rf "$tmp"
}
trap cleanup EXIT
maketmp() {
  if ! tmp=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/release-stage.XXXXXX"); then
    tmp=""
    if [ "$MODE" = verify ]; then
      fail question "cannot make a working directory; nothing was checked"
    fi
    fail failed "cannot make a working directory; nothing was released"
  fi
}

# version <outcome>: the bead's title's last word in $v (and the bead's
# show --json in $show), refused unless v<major>.<minor>.<patch>. A bad one
# is a question about the bead itself, for a person to fix; a bead that
# cannot be read reports <outcome>.
version() {
  show=$("$Y" bead show "$BEAD" --json) || fail "$1" "reading the bead failed; nothing was released"
  title=$(printf '%s' "$show" | jq -r '.title // empty')
  title=$(printf '%s' "$title" | sed 's/ *$//')
  v=${title##* }
  # grep matches line by line, so a title with a newline in its last word
  # is refused here first: only the version, whole, reaches a tag, gh or
  # the signing commands.
  case $v in *[!v0-9.]*) v_ok="" ;; *) v_ok=1 ;; esac
  if [ -z "$v_ok" ] || ! printf '%s\n' "$v" | grep -Eq '^v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$'; then
    fail question "the bead's title names no version (its last word is \"$v\"): title it \"$PRODUCT v<major>.<minor>.<patch>\" and advance the bead back to $MODE"
  fi
}

# actions_off: whether GitHub Actions is switched off on the release repo,
# asked of gh before build waits and before verify attests. Only a false
# answer counts: true, or an answer that cannot be read, is today's
# behaviour (wait for CI, check the attestations).
actions_off() {
  enabled=$(gh api "repos/$GHREPO/actions/permissions" --jq .enabled 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  [ "$enabled" = false ]
}

# name_of <line>: the file name a line of checksums.txt sums, read as verify
# reads one (the sum, white space, an optional binary mark, the name).
name_of() {
  printf '%s\n' "$1" | sed -n 's/^[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]*  *[* ]\{0,1\}\(.*\)$/\1/p'
}

# line_of <checksums file> <name>: the first line of the file that sums <name>.
line_of() {
  while IFS= read -r sumline; do
    if [ "$(name_of "$sumline")" = "$2" ]; then
      printf '%s\n' "$sumline"
      return 0
    fi
  done <"$1"
  return 1
}

# has_asset <release view json> <name>: whether the release holds an asset of
# that name. An answer that cannot be read holds none.
has_asset() {
  printf '%s' "$1" | jq -e --arg n "$2" '[.assets[]?.name] | index($n) != null' >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# darwin_archives: builds the two darwin archives of $v here, signed with the
# Developer ID and notarized by Apple (contrib/release/build does both, from
# the variables the exec group's env_file gives it), and adds them to the
# standing draft: its checksums.txt is made to name them beside CI's linux
# archives and its install.sh, and they go up with --clobber, so that a run
# again replaces what an earlier one left. CI's checksums.txt is fetched and
# added to, not made again: the sums of the linux archives stay CI's own, and
# verify checks them against the files before anything is published. $epoch,
# $tmp, $v, $REPO and $GHREPO are build's.
darwin_archives() {
  darwin="yardr_${v#v}_darwin_arm64.tar.gz yardr_${v#v}_darwin_amd64.tar.gz"
  mkdir -p "$tmp/dist" "$tmp/ci" "$tmp/merged" ||
    fail failed "cannot make a working directory; nothing was released"
  echo "== $BEAD: building the darwin archives of $v on this machine, signed and notarized (Apple's answer takes minutes)"
  if ! SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH="$epoch" YARDR_RELEASE_TARGETS="darwin/arm64 darwin/amd64" "$REPO/contrib/release/build" "$v" "$tmp/dist" >"$tmp/build.log" 2>&1; then
    fail failed "building the darwin archives of $v on this machine failed, and the draft holds CI's linux archives and no darwin one: fix the failure, then advance the bead back to build, which adopts the draft and builds them again" "$(tail -n 20 "$tmp/build.log")"
  fi
  for archive in $darwin; do
    [ -f "$tmp/dist/$archive" ] ||
      fail failed "the build on this machine did not make $archive; fix the failure, then advance the bead back to build" "$(tail -n 20 "$tmp/build.log")"
  done
  # The order contrib/release/build writes: the archives, then install.sh,
  # once. The two this machine made come from its own checksums, CI's lines
  # are all the others (but any of the two an earlier run left), and the
  # install.sh line is CI's, which this machine's must equal: the same tag
  # makes the same file, so a difference says the two did not build the
  # same commit.
  sums=$tmp/merged/checksums.txt
  : >"$sums"
  for archive in $darwin; do
    sumline=$(line_of "$tmp/dist/checksums.txt" "$archive") ||
      fail failed "the checksums of the build on this machine have no line for $archive; fix the failure, then advance the bead back to build"
    printf '%s\n' "$sumline" >>"$sums"
  done
  while IFS= read -r sumline; do
    [ -n "$sumline" ] || continue
    case " $darwin install.sh " in *" $(name_of "$sumline") "*) continue ;; esac
    printf '%s\n' "$sumline" >>"$sums"
  done <"$tmp/ci/checksums.txt"
  my_install=$(line_of "$tmp/dist/checksums.txt" install.sh) || my_install=""
  if [ -n "$my_install" ]; then
    [ -n "$ci_install" ] ||
      fail failed "CI's checksums.txt has no line for install.sh, so the draft's checksums would not cover the installer; advance the bead back to build"
    [ "$ci_install" = "$my_install" ] ||
      fail failed "CI summed an install.sh that is not the one this machine built $v with, so the two did not build the same files; delete the tag here and on $REMOTE and the draft, and advance the bead back to build" "CI: $ci_install
here: $my_install"
    printf '%s\n' "$ci_install" >>"$sums"
  elif [ -n "$ci_install" ]; then
    fail failed "CI's checksums.txt names install.sh, but the build on this machine did not make it; advance the bead back to build"
  fi
  set --
  for archive in $darwin; do
    set -- "$@" "$tmp/dist/$archive"
  done
  gh release upload "$v" --repo "$GHREPO" "$@" "$sums" --clobber >"$tmp/upload.log" 2>&1 ||
    fail failed "uploading the darwin archives and the whole checksums.txt to the draft for $v failed, and the draft may hold some of them: advance the bead back to build, which adopts the draft and does it again" "$(cat "$tmp/upload.log")"
}

build() {
  : "${YARDR_DEPOT_PATH:?the bead has no depot}" "${YARDR_BASE:?the bead has no base}"
  command -v git >/dev/null 2>&1 || fail failed "git is not on PATH; the release group runs where jq, git and gh are"
  command -v gh >/dev/null 2>&1 || fail failed "gh is not on PATH (and logged in); the release group runs where jq, git and gh are"
  version failed
  echo "== $BEAD: build $v of $GHREPO from $BASE"
  # The body is the release notes; an empty one would make a release that
  # says nothing.
  body=$(printf '%s' "$show" | jq -r '.body // empty')
  [ -n "$body" ] || fail question "the bead has no body, which is the release notes: write them on it and advance the bead back to build"
  # The darwin archives are built, signed and notarized here, whatever builds
  # the rest, and are never shipped unsigned. contrib/release/build only
  # warns when it is told nothing of signing, so this stops it before the tag
  # is made: after the tag CI has run, and a refusal then would leave a tag
  # and a draft for someone to delete.
  unset_vars=""
  [ -n "${YARDR_RELEASE_SIGN_IDENTITY:-}" ] || unset_vars="YARDR_RELEASE_SIGN_IDENTITY"
  [ -n "${YARDR_RELEASE_NOTARY_PROFILE:-}" ] || unset_vars="${unset_vars:+$unset_vars and }YARDR_RELEASE_NOTARY_PROFILE"
  [ -z "$unset_vars" ] ||
    fail failed "not set where this group runs: $unset_vars. The darwin archives are built here, signed and notarized, and never shipped unsigned: name both variables in a file only the operator can read (mode 0600, one NAME=value per line, the value unquoted) and give it to the group (yardr group set <group> env_file=<file>); docs/install.md, \"Signed darwin binaries\", has the lines. Then advance the bead back to build. Nothing was tagged."
  [ -z "$(git -C "$REPO" status --porcelain)" ] ||
    fail failed "$REPO has uncommitted or untracked files; commit or clean them, then advance the bead back to build"
  fetch_out=$(git -C "$REPO" fetch -q "$REMOTE" "$BASE" 2>&1) ||
    fail failed "fetching $REMOTE/$BASE in $REPO failed: $fetch_out. Nothing was tagged."
  base=$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse --verify -q "refs/remotes/$REMOTE/$BASE^{commit}") ||
    fail failed "$REMOTE/$BASE is not a branch $REPO knows after fetching; nothing was tagged"
  head=$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse --verify -q 'HEAD^{commit}') ||
    fail failed "reading HEAD in $REPO failed; nothing was tagged"
  [ "$head" = "$base" ] ||
    fail failed "$REPO is at $(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse --short "$head"), not at $REMOTE/$BASE ($(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse --short "$base")): check out $BASE there and pull, then advance the bead back to build"
  at=$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse --short "$base")
  # A run that died after tagging resumes: the tag is ours when it is at
  # this base, and tagging and pushing it again change nothing. A draft
  # already standing is a run that died while waiting, and is adopted,
  # not refused; anything published is.
  # No release standing reads as empty: gh fails, and jq passes empty
  # input through with success and no output.
  existing=$(gh release view "$v" --repo "$GHREPO" --json isDraft 2>/dev/null | jq -r .isDraft 2>/dev/null) || existing=""
  case $existing in
  "") ;;
  true) echo "== $BEAD: draft $v is already standing; adopting it" ;;
  false) fail failed "release $v already exists on $GHREPO, and is published: if a run left it half-made, fix it by hand and advance the bead to sign" ;;
  *) fail question "reading release $v failed; nothing was tagged" ;;
  esac
  tagat=$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse --verify -q "refs/tags/$v^{commit}" 2>/dev/null || true)
  if [ -n "$tagat" ] && [ "$tagat" != "$base" ]; then
    fail failed "tag $v already exists in $REPO, at another commit ($(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse --short "$tagat")): delete it or title the bead for another version"
  fi
  maketmp
  [ -z "$tagat" ] || echo "== $BEAD: tag $v is already at $at; finishing that run"
  [ -n "$tagat" ] || git -C "$REPO" tag "$v" "$base" || fail failed "tagging $at as $v failed; nothing was pushed"
  git -C "$REPO" push "$REMOTE" "$v" >"$tmp/push.log" 2>&1 ||
    fail failed "tag $v is made but pushing it to $REMOTE failed; push it by hand, then advance the bead back to build" "$(tail -n 10 "$tmp/push.log")"
  # Whatever is built on this machine is built reproducibly, from this
  # checkout at the pushed tag: SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH is the tagged commit's time,
  # as the workflow sets it.
  epoch=$(git -C "$REPO" log -1 --format=%ct "$base" 2>/dev/null) || epoch=""
  # With Actions off on the release repo there is no CI to make the draft:
  # all four archives are built on this machine instead. A draft already
  # standing is adopted here too, filled again rather than refused.
  if actions_off; then
    echo "== $BEAD: Actions is off on $GHREPO; building $v on this machine"
    mkdir -p "$tmp/dist" ||
      fail failed "cannot make a working directory; nothing was released"
    if ! SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH="$epoch" "$REPO/contrib/release/build" "$v" "$tmp/dist" >"$tmp/build.log" 2>&1; then
      fail failed "building $v on this machine failed; fix the failure, delete the tag here and on $REMOTE, and advance the bead back to build" "$(tail -n 20 "$tmp/build.log")"
    fi
    n_dist=0
    for f in "$tmp/dist"/*.tar.gz; do
      [ -f "$f" ] || continue
      n_dist=$((n_dist + 1))
    done
    missing=""
    for target in darwin_amd64 darwin_arm64 linux_amd64 linux_arm64; do
      archive="yardr_${v#v}_$target.tar.gz"
      [ -f "$tmp/dist/$archive" ] || missing="$missing $archive"
    done
    if [ "$n_dist" -ne 4 ] || [ -n "$missing" ] || [ ! -f "$tmp/dist/checksums.txt" ]; then
      fail failed "the build on this machine did not make the four expected archives and checksums.txt (found $n_dist archives; missing:$missing); fix the failure, delete the tag here and on $REMOTE, and advance the bead back to build" "$(tail -n 20 "$tmp/build.log")"
    fi
    # The installer rides beside the archives when the build wrote one;
    # another depot's release flow, which runs this script too, may have none.
    installer=""
    [ ! -f "$tmp/dist/install.sh" ] || installer="$tmp/dist/install.sh"
    printf '%s\n' "$body" >"$tmp/notes.md"
    if [ "$existing" = true ]; then
      gh release upload "$v" --repo "$GHREPO" "$tmp/dist"/*.tar.gz "$tmp/dist/checksums.txt" ${installer:+"$installer"} --clobber >"$tmp/upload.log" 2>&1 ||
        fail failed "uploading this machine's build to the standing draft for $v failed; build again by hand (contrib/release/build $v dist) and upload it, then advance the bead to sign" "$(cat "$tmp/upload.log")"
      if gh release edit "$v" --repo "$GHREPO" --notes-file "$tmp/notes.md" >"$tmp/notes.log" 2>&1; then
        echo "== $BEAD: the draft's notes are the bead's body"
      else
        fail failed "the draft for $v holds this machine's build but setting its notes from the bead failed; set them by hand from the bead's body, then advance the bead to sign" "$(cat "$tmp/notes.log")"
      fi
    else
      if gh release create "$v" --repo "$GHREPO" --draft --verify-tag --notes-file "$tmp/notes.md" "$tmp/dist"/*.tar.gz "$tmp/dist/checksums.txt" ${installer:+"$installer"} >"$tmp/create.log" 2>&1; then
        echo "== $BEAD: draft $v holds this machine's build"
      else
        fail failed "the build for $v is made but creating its draft failed; build again by hand (contrib/release/build $v dist) and make the draft from it, then advance the bead to sign" "$(cat "$tmp/create.log")"
      fi
    fi
    built_by="built on this machine from $at, the darwin binaries signed and notarized, with no attestation"
    run_line=""
  else
    # The pushed tag starts CI's release workflow, which tests, builds the
    # linux archives and checksums.txt, attests them and makes the draft. Each
    # round asks the workflow for its run and the release for its assets;
    # the draft is done when it holds the two linux archives, by name, and
    # checksums.txt, plus any installer that file names. The darwin archives
    # are not CI's to make.
    # A gh answer that cannot be read is another round, not a failure: only
    # a run that concluded, or the last round, ends the wait.
    rounds=${YARDR_RELEASE_WAIT_ROUNDS:-60}
    sleep_for=${YARDR_RELEASE_WAIT_SLEEP:-30}
    case $rounds in '' | *[!0-9]*) rounds=60 ;; esac
    case $sleep_for in '' | *[!0-9]*) sleep_for=30 ;; esac
    linux_archives="yardr_${v#v}_linux_amd64.tar.gz yardr_${v#v}_linux_arm64.tar.gz"
    ci_assets="$linux_archives checksums.txt"
    run_url=""
    ready=""
    ci_install=""
    i=0
    while [ "$i" -lt "$rounds" ]; do
      i=$((i + 1))
      runs=$(gh run list --repo "$GHREPO" --workflow release.yml --commit "$base" --event push --json conclusion,status,url --limit 1 2>/dev/null) || runs=""
      if [ -n "$runs" ]; then
        status=$(printf '%s' "$runs" | jq -r '.[0].status // empty' 2>/dev/null) || status=""
        concl=$(printf '%s' "$runs" | jq -r '.[0].conclusion // empty' 2>/dev/null) || concl=""
        url=$(printf '%s' "$runs" | jq -r '.[0].url // empty' 2>/dev/null) || url=""
        [ -z "$url" ] || run_url=$url
        if [ "$status" = completed ] && [ -n "$concl" ] && [ "$concl" != success ]; then
          fail failed "CI's release run for $v $concl ($run_url): fix the failure, delete the tag here and on $REMOTE, and advance the bead back to build"
        fi
      fi
      draft=$(gh release view "$v" --repo "$GHREPO" --json assets 2>/dev/null) || draft=""
      ready=1
      for name in $ci_assets; do
        has_asset "$draft" "$name" || ready=""
      done
      if [ -n "$ready" ]; then
        mkdir -p "$tmp/ci" ||
          fail failed "cannot make a working directory; nothing was released"
        gh release download "$v" --repo "$GHREPO" --pattern checksums.txt --dir "$tmp/ci" --clobber >"$tmp/download.log" 2>&1 ||
          fail failed "downloading CI's checksums.txt of $v failed, so the darwin archives were not added to the draft; advance the bead back to build" "$(cat "$tmp/download.log")"
        [ -f "$tmp/ci/checksums.txt" ] ||
          fail failed "draft $v holds no checksums.txt to add the darwin archives to; advance the bead back to build"
        for archive in $linux_archives; do
          line_of "$tmp/ci/checksums.txt" "$archive" >/dev/null ||
            fail failed "CI's checksums.txt has no line for $archive, so the draft's checksums would not cover every archive; advance the bead back to build"
        done
        ci_install=$(line_of "$tmp/ci/checksums.txt" install.sh) || ci_install=""
        if [ -f "$REPO/install.sh" ] && [ -z "$ci_install" ]; then
          fail failed "CI's checksums.txt has no line for install.sh, so the draft's checksums would not cover the installer; advance the bead back to build"
        fi
        if [ -n "$ci_install" ]; then
          has_asset "$draft" install.sh || ready=""
        fi
      fi
      if [ -n "$ready" ]; then
        echo "== $BEAD: draft $v holds CI's linux archives, checksums.txt and every file it sums"
        break
      fi
      [ "$i" -ge "$rounds" ] || sleep "$sleep_for"
    done
    if [ -z "$ready" ]; then
      mins=$((rounds * sleep_for / 60))
      where="no release run has shown for the tag yet"
      [ -z "$run_url" ] || where="the run is at $run_url"
      fail failed "CI did not finish the draft for $v in $mins minutes ($where); check the run, and to run it again delete the tag here and on $REMOTE and advance the bead back to build"
    fi
    darwin_archives
    printf '%s\n' "$body" >"$tmp/notes.md"
    if gh release edit "$v" --repo "$GHREPO" --notes-file "$tmp/notes.md" >"$tmp/notes.log" 2>&1; then
      echo "== $BEAD: the draft's notes are the bead's body"
    else
      fail failed "the draft for $v is complete but setting its notes from the bead failed; set them by hand from the bead's body, then advance the bead to sign" "$(cat "$tmp/notes.log")"
    fi
    built_by="the linux archives built by CI from $at, the darwin archives built, signed and notarized on this machine"
    run_line=""
    [ -z "$run_url" ] || run_line=" CI's run is at $run_url."
  fi
  url="https://github.com/$GHREPO/releases/tag/$v"
  # A literal ~ for the note below: mckean pastes those commands into a
  # shell of their own, where the tilde expands to their home, not this one's.
  # shellcheck disable=SC2088
  dir="~/releases/$v"
  done_with sign "build: $PRODUCT $v is drafted ($url, $built_by): the four archives and checksums.txt, the checksums not yet signed.$run_line Sign them with the five commands, in order (minisign asks for the key's password; each names $v):

mkdir -p $dir
gh release download $v --repo $GHREPO --pattern checksums.txt --dir $dir --clobber
minisign -S -s $KEYPATH -m $dir/checksums.txt
minisign -V -P $PUBKEY -m $dir/checksums.txt
gh release upload $v --repo $GHREPO $dir/checksums.txt.minisig --clobber

Then Done: the verify stage checks the signature next."
}

# tagged_installer: the install.sh the tag $v holds, on stdout; fails where the
# depot is not known, the tag is not in it, or the tag has none. git -C with an
# empty path would stay in the current directory, hence the test first.
tagged_installer() {
  [ -n "$REPO" ] && git -C "$REPO" show "refs/tags/$v:install.sh" 2>/dev/null
}

# darwin_check: each darwin archive of the downloaded release ($archives, in
# $tmp), looked at as a Mac that takes it would, in place of an attestation:
# this machine built them, and nothing but their signature says so. The
# archive's yardr is unpacked alone, into a directory of its own, and must
# pass codesign --verify --strict, be signed by the identity the group signs
# with (the Authority line of codesign -dv, which an ad hoc signature, as the
# Go linker leaves one, does not have), and be taken by Gatekeeper as a
# notarized Developer ID binary (spctl). The assessment asked of spctl is
# install's, not exec's: exec is of apps, and refuses a notarized bare binary
# as "the code is valid but does not seem to be an app", while install judges
# the signature and the notarization alone. A bare binary carries no ticket,
# so spctl asks Apple for it, and the check needs the network. A fault is a
# question, as an archive that does not match is: the release is remade by
# hand. Sets $mac to the sentence the done note carries: what was checked, or
# that there was nothing to check, so that a skipped check is never silent.
darwin_check() {
  signed=""
  for b in $archives; do
    case $b in *_darwin_*.tar.gz) signed="$signed $b" ;; esac
  done
  if [ -z "$signed" ]; then
    mac="The release holds no darwin archive, so none was checked for a signature."
    return
  fi
  identity=${YARDR_RELEASE_SIGN_IDENTITY:-}
  [ -n "$identity" ] ||
    fail question "release $v holds darwin archives, and YARDR_RELEASE_SIGN_IDENTITY is not set where this group runs, so there is no identity to hold their signature to: name it in the group's env_file (docs/install.md, \"Signed darwin binaries\"), then Done again"
  remade="it is remade by hand (the release and the tag deleted, the bead back to build)"
  checked=""
  for b in $signed; do
    dest=$tmp/mac-${b%.tar.gz}
    mkdir "$dest" || fail question "cannot make a working directory; the darwin archives were not checked"
    tar -xzf "$tmp/$b" -C "$dest" yardr >"$tmp/mac.log" 2>&1 ||
      fail question "unpacking the yardr of $b from release $v failed (the archive holds none, or tar could not read it), so the darwin archives were not checked; $remade" "$(cat "$tmp/mac.log")"
    out=$(codesign --verify --strict --verbose=2 "$dest/yardr" 2>&1) ||
      fail question "codesign --verify refuses the yardr in $b of release $v (it is not signed, or codesign could not run); $remade" "$out"
    out=$(codesign -dv --verbose=4 "$dest/yardr" 2>&1) ||
      fail question "codesign -dv could not read the signature of the yardr in $b of release $v; $remade" "$out"
    printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -q -x -F "Authority=$identity" ||
      fail question "the yardr in $b of release $v is not signed with $identity (codesign -dv names no such Authority, as it names none for an ad hoc signature); $remade" "$out"
    out=$(spctl -a -vv -t install "$dest/yardr" 2>&1) ||
      fail question "Gatekeeper refuses the yardr in $b of release $v (spctl -a -vv -t install failed: it is not notarized, or the check could not reach Apple); $remade" "$out"
    case $out in
    *"source=Notarized Developer ID"*) ;;
    *) fail question "Gatekeeper does not take the yardr in $b of release $v as notarized (spctl does not name source=Notarized Developer ID); $remade" "$out" ;;
    esac
    checked="${checked:+$checked, }$b"
  done
  mac="Each darwin archive's yardr is signed with $identity and notarized (codesign --verify passes, codesign -dv names the identity, and spctl names Notarized Developer ID as the source): $checked."
}

verify() {
  command -v gh >/dev/null 2>&1 || fail question "gh is not on PATH (and logged in); the release group runs where jq, gh and minisign are"
  command -v minisign >/dev/null 2>&1 || fail question "minisign is not on PATH; the release group runs where jq, gh and minisign are"
  if command -v shasum >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    sum="shasum -a 256"
  elif command -v sha256sum >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    sum="sha256sum"
  else
    fail question "neither shasum nor sha256sum is on PATH; the release group runs where one of them is"
  fi
  version question
  echo "== $BEAD: verify $v of $GHREPO"
  maketmp
  gh release download "$v" --repo "$GHREPO" --dir "$tmp" >"$tmp/download.log" 2>&1 ||
    fail question "downloading release $v failed; nothing was checked" "$(cat "$tmp/download.log")"
  [ -f "$tmp/checksums.txt" ] || fail question "release $v holds no checksums.txt; it is remade by hand (the release and the tag deleted, the bead back to build)"
  [ -f "$tmp/checksums.txt.minisig" ] ||
    fail failed "checksums.txt.minisig is not on release $v yet: run the five commands in the build's note, then Done again"
  # The signature covers the checksums, and the checksums cover each
  # archive; both are checked, in that order.
  minisign_out=$(minisign -V -P "$PUBKEY" -m "$tmp/checksums.txt" 2>&1) ||
    fail failed "the signature on release $v is bad" "$minisign_out"
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086 # $sum is a list
  (cd "$tmp" && $sum -c checksums.txt >check.log 2>&1) ||
    fail question "the files of release $v do not match its checksums.txt; it is remade by hand (the release and the tag deleted, the bead back to build)" "$(cat "$tmp/check.log")"
  names=$(sed -n 's/^[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]*  *[* ]\{0,1\}\(.*\)$/\1/p' "$tmp/checksums.txt")
  # The installer is no archive: where the checksums name it, the check above
  # has matched its bytes, and the tag, below, says which bytes they should be.
  # So it is neither counted nor attested with the archives.
  archives=$(printf '%s\n' "$names" | grep -v -x -F install.sh)
  n=$(printf '%s\n' "$archives" | grep -c .) || n=0
  [ "$n" -gt 0 ] || fail question "release $v's checksums.txt names no archives; it is remade by hand (the release and the tag deleted, the bead back to build)"
  # And nothing rides along that the checksums do not cover.
  extra=""
  for f in "$tmp"/*; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    b=${f##*/}
    case $b in
    checksums.txt | checksums.txt.minisig | download.log | check.log) continue ;;
    install.sh) continue ;; # held to the tag below, whether a line names it or not
    esac
    printf '%s\n' "$names" | grep -q -x -F "$b" || extra="$extra $b"
  done
  [ -z "$extra" ] ||
    fail question "release $v holds files its checksums.txt does not name ($extra); it is remade by hand (the release and the tag deleted, the bead back to build)"
  # No attestation reaches the installer, so besides its line in the signed
  # checksums what vouches for it is the tag: the release holds the tagged
  # commit's own install.sh, and holds it when the tag has one. A release made
  # before the checksums named it has the file and no line: it passes, with a
  # note, as it did.
  installer_note=""
  if [ -f "$tmp/install.sh" ]; then
    { tagged_installer >"$tmp/tagged-install.sh" && cmp -s "$tmp/tagged-install.sh" "$tmp/install.sh"; } ||
      fail question "release $v holds an install.sh that is not tag $v's own (compared in ${REPO:-no depot}); it is remade by hand (the release and the tag deleted, the bead back to build)"
    if printf '%s\n' "$names" | grep -q -x -F install.sh; then
      installer_note=" Its install.sh matches its line in checksums.txt and is tag $v's own."
    else
      installer_note=" Its install.sh is tag $v's own, but checksums.txt has no line for it."
    fi
  elif tagged_installer >/dev/null; then
    fail question "tag $v has an install.sh and release $v holds none; it is remade by hand (the release and the tag deleted, the bead back to build)"
  fi
  # The darwin archives are this machine's, whether or not Actions is on, so
  # they are checked by their signature either way.
  darwin_check
  # With Actions off the release was built on this machine, with no
  # attestation to check; the signature, the checksums and the darwin
  # binaries' signatures are the whole of it.
  if actions_off; then
    echo "== $BEAD: Actions is off on $GHREPO; skipping attestations"
    done_with publish "verify: the signature on $v is good ($minisign_out), its $n archives match checksums.txt (no attestation: built on this machine).$installer_note $mac"
  fi
  # Each of CI's archives has an attestation that says CI's release workflow
  # built it; without one the files are not the workflow's, however well they
  # checksum. The workflow attests the archives it builds, the linux ones, and
  # neither the darwin archives, which darwin_check has looked at, nor the
  # installer, so those are not asked.
  attested=0
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086 # $archives is a newline-separated list
  for b in $archives; do
    case $b in *_darwin_*.tar.gz) continue ;; esac
    attest_out=$(gh attestation verify "$tmp/$b" --repo "$GHREPO" \
      --signer-workflow "$GHREPO/.github/workflows/release.yml" \
      --source-ref "refs/tags/$v" 2>&1) ||
      fail question "the attestation for $b on release $v is missing or bad: the files are not CI's build, or the check could not reach GitHub. It is remade by hand (the release and the tag deleted, the bead back to build), or checked again where the failure was the network" "$attest_out"
    attested=$((attested + 1))
  done
  done_with publish "verify: the signature on $v is good ($minisign_out), its $n archives match checksums.txt, and the $attested archives CI built have attestations that verify.$installer_note $mac"
}

publish() {
  : "${YARDR_DEPOT_PATH:?the bead has no depot}"
  command -v git >/dev/null 2>&1 || fail failed "git is not on PATH; the release group runs where jq, git and gh are"
  command -v gh >/dev/null 2>&1 || fail failed "gh is not on PATH (and logged in); the release group runs where jq, git and gh are"
  version failed
  echo "== $BEAD: publish $v of $GHREPO"
  maketmp
  at=$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse --short --verify -q "refs/tags/$v^{commit}" 2>/dev/null) ||
    fail failed "tag $v is not in $REPO; fetch it, then advance the bead back to publish"
  gh release edit "$v" --repo "$GHREPO" --draft=false >"$tmp/draft.log" 2>&1 ||
    fail failed "releasing the draft for $v failed; release it by hand (gh release edit $v --repo $GHREPO --draft=false), then advance the bead back to publish" "$(cat "$tmp/draft.log")"
  url="https://github.com/$GHREPO/releases/tag/$v"
  day=$(date +%Y-%m-%d)
  if [ "$PRODUCT" = yardr ]; then other=footplate; else other=yardr; fi
  # The sync writes the release directory and latest.json, and for a yardr
  # release whose tag holds an install.sh, public/install.sh too: the filed
  # bead names it as the one other path, and its Done when allows it.
  writes="replaces the previous $PRODUCT release directory with $v/ and rewrites public/releases/latest.json"
  shows="shows only public/releases/"
  if [ "$PRODUCT" = yardr ] && tagged_installer >/dev/null; then
    writes="$writes and copies the release's install.sh to public/install.sh"
    shows="shows only public/releases/ and public/install.sh"
  fi
  title="Sync $PRODUCT $v to yardr.dev: scripts/sync-release $PRODUCT $v"
  body="$PRODUCT $v is released ($url, built from $at) and its checksums.txt.minisig is uploaded and verified against the release key on $day.

Run, from the depot's checkout: \`scripts/sync-release $PRODUCT $v\` (fetches the release with gh from $GHREPO, verifies the signature and the four archives, $writes). Commit what it wrote. Nothing else changes.

Done when: the depot's test command passes; \`git diff main --stat\` $shows (the $PRODUCT paths, not $other's). After the deploy, https://yardr.dev/releases/latest.json names $v and the page offers it."
  new=$(printf '%s' "$body" | "$Y" bead create "$title" -d yardr.dev -p 1 -b - -l difficulty:1,size:small --json 2>"$tmp/create.err" | jq -r '.id // empty') || new=""
  [ -n "$new" ] || fail failed "filing the sync bead failed" "$(cat "$tmp/create.err")"
  "$Y" bead advance "$new" --to new || fail failed "sync bead $new is filed but advancing it to new failed; advance it by hand, then this bead to published"
  "$Y" bead note "$BEAD" "publish: filed $new (\"$title\") and advanced it to new."
  done_with published "publish: $PRODUCT $v is released from its draft; $new carries the site's sync."
}

case "$MODE" in
build) build ;;
verify) verify ;;
publish) publish ;;
esac
